```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/")
async def read_root():
    return {"Hello": "World"}

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(item_id: int):
    return {"item_id": item_id}

@app.put("/items/{item_id}")
async def create_item(item_id: int, item: dict):
    return {"item_id": item_id, **item}

@app.delete("/items/{item_id}")
async def delete_item(item_id: int):
    return {"result": "success", "item_id": item_id}
```

You can run this code in an environment where FastAPI and Uvicorn are installed. Save the code to a file named `main.py` and run it using the command `uvicorn main:app --reload`. This will start a minimal FastAPI server on `http://127.0.0.1:8000`. Accessing the root URL will return a JSON response with `{"Hello": "World"}`. You can also test the other endpoints to see the basic CRUD operations in action.

